    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, welcome. What brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S020",
      "patient_response": "Well, I came in because... I mean, it's mostly the voices. I keep hearing people talking about me. It usually happens when I'm in the office, like through the conference room walls, or even from my phone when it's completely powered off. It's usually the board members or some of the senior staff, and they're just constantly saying I'm ruining the company, that I don't know what I'm doing. It sounds so real, so clear, that I actually catch myself answering them out loud before I realize nobody else in the room heard a thing. \n\nWhich is just... it's exhausting. And honestly, it reminds me of this time when I was in college, before I dropped out to start my first business. I had this professor, Dr. Aris, who taught macroeconomics, and he would always mutter under his breath while he was writing on the chalkboard. You could never quite make out what he was saying, but you just knew it was something condescending. I used to sit in the second row, right by the window—it was a beautiful old brick building, actually, ivy growing all up the side of it. Sometimes I'd just watch the squirrels outside instead of paying attention. They're such busy little things, aren't they? Always preparing for something. But anyway, yeah, the board members... they're just so loud lately.",
